  <h2>Frequently asked questions</h2>
  <details class="faq-item" open><summary>Is a high yield always a trap?</summary><div class="faq-answer">Not always. REITs, MLPs, BDCs, and business-development companies are required by their tax structure to distribute most of their income, so double-digit yields are normal. For typical operating companies (consumer, industrial, tech), a yield above 8% almost always signals the market expects a cut, a price decline, or both.</div></details>
  <details class="faq-item"><summary>What's a healthy payout ratio?</summary><div class="faq-answer">For most US companies, under 70% is comfortable. For REITs and MLPs, the right metric is not payout ratio on GAAP earnings but AFFO coverage or distributable cash flow coverage. See the sectors section above.</div></details>
  <details class="faq-item"><summary>Should I care about the dividend yield or the total return?</summary><div class="faq-answer">Total return, always. Yield is one component of total return (the other being price change), and a high yield can mask a falling price. A stock yielding 8% that loses 12% in price delivers −4% for the year. Compare total return over 3, 5, and 10 years.</div></details>
  <details class="faq-item"><summary>Are Dividend Aristocrats safe?</summary><div class="faq-answer">Safer than non-Aristocrats, not safe. The Aristocrats list (25+ years of consecutive increases) is a quality screen, not a guarantee. General Electric was on the list until 2009 and AT&amp;T cut after 36 years in 2022. Treat it as a starting filter, not a final answer.</div></details>

